<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Huastec chiefdoms</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The independent lordships of the Huastec (Teenek) people on the north-east Gulf Coast of Mexico, speakers of a Maya language long separated from the rest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people">Huastec</a> separated from other Maya speakers well over two thousand years ago and settled the lowlands of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>. From around 900 their region was organised into numerous small lordships centred on towns with circular platforms.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each lordship was led by its own chief, and there was no unified state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people">Huastec</a> were known for shell ornaments, distinctive black-on-white pottery and sculptures of youthful deities such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huastec-adolescent">Huastec Adolescent</a>. The fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and textiles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> sources viewed them as foreign and depicted them with prejudice.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1450s the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> conquered much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> and demanded tribute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> subdued the region in 1522, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a> devastated it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people">Teenek</a> people survive and their language is still spoken.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 900 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.61%;width:12.38%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Huastec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Huastec</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Huaxteca, Huastecs, Teenek</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1211&amp;focus=huastec-chiefdoms" aria-label="Huastec chiefdoms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1952 1309 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Huastec chiefdoms lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2322 1714h0M4910 989h0M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2272 1565h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2272 1565h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mexico. <a href="/atlas?year=1211&amp;focus=huastec-chiefdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
